.Dd September 21, 2026
.Dt SFIND 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sfind
.Nd find(1) backed by the Spotlight index
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl H | L | P
.Op Fl EXdsx
.Op Fl f Ar path
.Op Fl -walk Ns Op = Ns Ar mode
.Op Fl -progress
.Ar path ...
.Op Fl -expr Ar string
.Op Ar expression
.Nm
.Fl -mdfind
.Ar path ...
.Op Ar expression
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
evaluates
.Xr find 1
expressions against the macOS Spotlight index instead of walking the
filesystem.
On indexed trees, searches that would visit millions of inodes return in
milliseconds.
.Pp
BSD
.Xr find 1
semantics are ground truth: the expression grammar, primaries, operators,
numeric argument forms
.Pq Ar +n , -n , n ,
time rounding, size rounding, symlink modes
.Pq Fl H | L | P ,
the implicit
.Ic -print
rule, and exit status all match
.Pa /usr/bin/find
and are verified against it by the test suite.
Conflict-free GNU extensions are also accepted:
.Ic -printf , -regextype , -readable , -writable , -executable , -daystart ,
and the
.Ic -perm Ar /mode
spelling.
.Pp
Internally the expression is translated into a Spotlight query that
over-approximates it
.Pq the index narrows ,
and every candidate is then re-verified exactly with
.Xr lstat 2 ,
.Xr fnmatch 3 ,
and
.Xr regexec 3
before actions run
.Pq the post-filter decides .
Correctness never depends on the index; only completeness does
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS .
.Sh SFIND OPTIONS
In addition to the
.Xr find 1
options
.Fl H , L , P , E , X , d , f , s ,
and
.Fl x ,
.Nm
accepts double-dash flags
.Pq a namespace find never uses :
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl -expr Ar string
Supply expression tokens as a single string.
Whitespace splits tokens; single quotes, double quotes, and backslashes
group and escape; unquoted parentheses are self-delimiting; an unquoted
.Ql !\&
is self-delimiting only where a new token would start, so fnmatch classes
like
.Ql [!a]*
survive.
No variable expansion, globbing, or substitution is performed.
The result is spliced into the expression where the flag appears and may be
mixed with discrete tokens:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~/Docs --expr '(-name "*.md" -o -name "*.txt") -mtime -7'
.Ed
.It Fl -mdfind
Do not run the search; instead print the equivalent
.Xr mdfind 1
invocation for the translated Spotlight query.
Expression terms the query language cannot express are reported to standard
error, since the printed query then returns a superset of the expression's
matches.
.It Fl -walk Ns Op = Ns Ar mode
Supplement the index with a filesystem walk.
.Ar mode
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "gaps" -compact
.It Cm off
The default: only the index is consulted and no directory is ever read.
.It Cm gaps
The default for a bare
.Fl -walk .
The walk visits only what Spotlight does not index: entries whose names start
with a dot, anything that is not a regular file or directory
.Pq symlinks, FIFOs, sockets, devices ,
everything under a dot directory, a
.Pa *.noindex
directory, a directory carrying
.Pa .metadata_never_index ,
or an application bundle, every directory missing from the index's own list of
the folders it holds under the roots
.Pq privacy exclusions, unindexed volumes, most of Pa ~/Library ,
and any search root the index holds nothing for.
The index still answers for everything else, and regular files that are the
index's job cost the walk no
.Xr stat 2
at all, so
.Nm
remains faster than
.Xr find 1 .
Small scopes are walked outright, before the index is consulted, and skip its
startup cost entirely.
.It Cm only
Never consult the index: a plain filesystem walk, equivalent to
.Xr find 1 .
Use this when the index may be stale or the intent is to find something rather
than to query Spotlight.
.Ic -content
matches nothing in this mode.
.El
.Pp
The walk honors
.Fl H , L , P , x ,
and
.Fl d ,
.Ic -maxdepth
.Pq deeper directories are never read ,
and
.Ic -prune
.Po pruned subtrees are never read unless the expression contains an
.Ic -exec
family primary, whose exit status could steer evaluation
.Pc .
When
.Ic -path
or
.Ic -regex
names a literal directory component
.Pq for example Ql */node_modules/* ,
the index is first asked for directories of that name under the roots and both
the query and the walk are confined to those subtrees.
Under either walk mode the completeness warnings described in
.Sx CAVEATS
are not printed.
.It Fl -progress
Show a progress line on standard error.
On a terminal it is redrawn in place and cleared before other output;
otherwise a status line is emitted about once a second.
It shows a bar with a best-effort completion estimate
.Pq indeterminate during the index phase, which reports no total ,
the current phase, and counters: candidates from the index query, candidates
from the walk, entries the walk scanned, candidates the post-filter rejected,
matches, and elapsed time.
A summary line is printed at the end.
.It Fl -help , Fl \&?
Print usage and a summary.
.It Fl -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh SFIND PRIMARIES
All
.Xr find 1
primaries and operators are accepted.
.Nm
adds one:
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Ic -content Ar words
True if the file's Spotlight-indexed text contains every whitespace-separated
word in
.Ar words ,
compared case- and diacritic-insensitively.
A bare word matches whole words only;
.Ql *
extends it to prefixes, suffixes, and substrings
.Pq Ql invoic* ;
a multi-word argument requires each word somewhere in the text, not the phrase.
Only the index can answer this primary: files the index does not hold never
match.
Under
.Fl -walk
each term is resolved by a dedicated index query over the search roots before
the search runs, and under
.Fl -walk Ns = Ns Cm only
it matches nothing.
Text content is not served for a search root that is itself a hidden
directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
As with
.Xr find 1 ,
the match count does not affect the exit status; errors
.Pq missing operands, unreadable files, failed Fl exec Ar ... No + children
do.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Markdown files changed this week:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~ -name '*.md' -mtime -7
.Ed
.Pp
Large downloads, sorted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind -s ~/Downloads -size +100M -type f
.Ed
.Pp
Show the Spotlight query that would run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~ --mdfind -name '*.swift' -newermt 'yesterday'
.Ed
.Pp
Dotfiles, which the index never returns, with the index still narrowing the
rest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~ --walk -name '*.env'
.Ed
.Pp
Symlinks, with a progress line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~ --walk --progress -type l
.Ed
.Pp
Full-text search over indexed content:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind ~/Documents -content invoice -mtime -30
.Ed
.Pp
A plain walk, as
.Xr find 1
would do it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfind --walk=only ~/Library -name '*.plist'
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr find 1 ,
.Xr mdfind 1 ,
.Xr mdutil 1
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/dnesting/sfind
— SPEC.md documents every option's index-vs-post-filter determination and
the verified behavioral details.
.Sh CAVEATS
Spotlight does not index everything, and by default
.Nm
never falls back to walking the filesystem.
Invisible to the index, and therefore to
.Nm
without
.Fl -walk :
dotfiles, symlinks, sockets, FIFOs, device nodes, application bundle
contents,
.Pa /usr , /bin , /etc , /tmp ,
volumes with indexing disabled, and any tree under a
.Pa .metadata_never_index
marker or
.Pa *.noindex
directory.
.Nm
warns on standard error when an expression term provably requires such files
.Pq for example Ic -type l ,
and, after an empty result, when a search root turns out to be excluded from
the index.
.Fl -walk
fills these gaps with a walk confined to them.
.Pp
The gap walk asks the index which directories it holds and walks the rest
exhaustively, but it cannot see an individual regular file the index lacks
inside a directory it does hold
.Pq a stale index ;
such files are still missed under
.Fl -walk Ns = Ns Cm gaps ,
and
.Fl -walk Ns = Ns Cm only
is the escape hatch.
When a
.Ic -path
or
.Ic -regex
component confines the search to index-found directories, such directories
inside hidden or excluded trees are invisible to the index and are not walked.
.Pp
Result ordering is unspecified
.Pq the index returns results in arbitrary order ;
use
.Fl s
for deterministic, find-compatible ordering.
.Fl atime
and
.Fl ctime
are evaluated exactly but cannot narrow the query
.Pq Spotlight indexes neither .
